sections.content:
**`@Query` JPQL:**

```java
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.customer WHERE o.status = :status")
List<Order> findByStatusWithCustomer(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);
```

- Named parameters preferred over positional `?1` — readable, refactor-friendly.

**`@Query` native SQL with pagination:**

```java
@Query(value = "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ?1 AND created_at > ?2",
       countQuery = "SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ?1 AND created_at > ?2",
       nativeQuery = true)
Page<Order> findRecentByCustomer(Long cid, Instant since, Pageable pageable);
```

- Native bypasses JPQL translation; pagination requires explicit `countQuery`.

**Constructor expression for DTO projection:**

```java
public record OrderSummary(Long id, String status, BigDecimal total) {}

@Query("SELECT new com.acme.OrderSummary(o.id, o.status, SUM(oi.price)) "
     + "FROM Order o JOIN o.items oi "
     + "WHERE o.createdAt > :since GROUP BY o.id, o.status")
List<OrderSummary> summarizeSince(Instant since);
```

**Interface-based projection:**

```java
public interface OrderSummaryView {
    Long getId();
    String getStatus();
    BigDecimal getTotal();
}

@Query("SELECT o.id AS id, o.status AS status, SUM(oi.price) AS total "
     + "FROM Order o JOIN o.items oi GROUP BY o.id, o.status")
List<OrderSummaryView> summariesAsInterface();
```

**Dynamic projection:**

```java
<T> List<T> findByStatus(OrderStatus status, Class<T> type);

List<OrderSummaryView> views = repo.findByStatus(PAID, OrderSummaryView.class);
List<Order> entities = repo.findByStatus(PAID, Order.class);
```

**`@Modifying` for UPDATE/DELETE:**

```java
@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true, flushAutomatically = true)
@Query("UPDATE Order o SET o.status = 'ARCHIVED' WHERE o.createdAt < :cutoff")
int archiveOld(Instant cutoff);
```

- `clearAutomatically` clears L1 after.
- `flushAutomatically` flushes pending writes before.
- Returns `int`/`long` rows affected; `void` works too.

**Specification for optional filters:**

```java
public class OrderSpecs {
    public static Specification<Order> hasCustomer(Long id) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> id == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("customerId"), id);
    }
    public static Specification<Order> hasStatus(OrderStatus s) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> s == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("status"), s);
    }
    public static Specification<Order> createdBetween(Instant from, Instant to) {
        return (root, q, cb) -> {
            if (from == null && to == null) return null;
            if (from == null) return cb.lessThanOrEqualTo(root.get("createdAt"), to);
            if (to == null)   return cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("createdAt"), from);
            return cb.between(root.get("createdAt"), from, to);
        };
    }
}

public Page<Order> search(Long cid, OrderStatus s, Instant from, Instant to, Pageable p) {
    Specification<Order> spec = Specification
        .where(OrderSpecs.hasCustomer(cid))
        .and(OrderSpecs.hasStatus(s))
        .and(OrderSpecs.createdBetween(from, to));
    return orderRepo.findAll(spec, p);
}
```

- Returning `null` from a Specification means 'no contribution to WHERE' — perfect for optional filters.

**Query By Example:**

```java
Order probe = new Order();
probe.setStatus(OrderStatus.PAID);
probe.setCustomerId(42L);
Example<Order> example = Example.of(probe,
    ExampleMatcher.matchingAll().withIgnorePaths("id", "amount"));
List<Order> results = orderRepo.findAll(example);
```

- Convenient for simple searches; weaker than Specifications for complex OR / comparisons.

**Entity graphs:**

```java
@Entity
@NamedEntityGraph(name = "Order.withItems",
    attributeNodes = @NamedAttributeNode("items"))
public class Order { ... }

@EntityGraph("Order.withItems")
List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus s);
```

- Declarative fetch plans without writing JPQL.
- Combines beautifully with derived queries — zero JPQL needed.

**When derived queries help:**
- Up to 3-4 parameters, names stay readable.
- Compile-time safety against entity field renames (Spring fails at startup).

**When derived queries hurt:**
- Optional filters -> switch to Specifications.
- Complex joins / aggregates -> switch to `@Query`.
- Many parameters -> method names become unreadable.

**Auditing setup:**

```java
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class BaseEntity {
    @CreatedDate Instant createdAt;
    @LastModifiedDate Instant updatedAt;
    @CreatedBy String createdBy;
    @LastModifiedBy String modifiedBy;
    @Version Long version;
}

@EnableJpaAuditing
public class JpaConfig {
    @Bean AuditorAware<String> auditorAware() {
        return () -> Optional.of(SecurityContextHolder.getContext()
            .getAuthentication().getName());
    }
}
```

**Transactions:**
- Repository methods are transactional by default for writes.
- Reads wrapped in `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` internally where supported.
- Compose at service layer around multiple repository calls.
- Avoid annotating entity classes or private methods.

**Watch for — common pitfalls:**
- **N+1** with derived queries + lazy associations -> `@EntityGraph`, `JOIN FETCH`, or DTO projections.
- **`LazyInitializationException`** — accessing lazy collection outside transaction; use entity graph or DTO.
- **`save()` semantics** — actually does `persist` or `merge` depending on ID; use `em.persist`/`em.merge` directly when semantics matter.
- **`Page` runs COUNT** — slow on huge tables; use `Slice` or cursor-based pagination.
- **`@Modifying` forgotten** — UPDATE/DELETE `@Query` throws `InvalidDataAccessApiUsageException`.
- **Native queries lose Pageable support** unless you supply `countQuery`.
- **Returning entities from controllers** leaks schema; use DTOs.
- **`nativeQuery = true`** bypasses JPQL parsing, loses type checking, breaks portability.

**Testing:**
- `@DataJpaTest` + `@Autowired TestEntityManager` for repository tests.
- Testcontainers + `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = NONE)` for real-DB integration tests.
- `@DynamicPropertySource` registers DB URL from the container.

**Observability:**
- `logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG`.
- `hibernate.format_sql=true`, `hibernate.highlight_sql=true` (dev only).
- `datasource-proxy` or `p6spy` for query timing with stack traces.
- Micrometer: `hibernate.sessions.open`, `jpa.query.duration`.

**Hibernate 6 / Spring Data JPA 3.x updates:**
- Stricter JPQL parser (catches previously tolerated bugs).
- `Stream<Entity>` for streaming queries.
- Improved `CriteriaBuilder` ergonomics.
- Native UUID type support.
- Window functions in JPQL.
- Better batch / streaming.

**Standard repository — extends `JpaRepository`:**

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status);
}
```

**Narrow API — only what you want exposed:**

```java
public interface OrderLookup extends Repository<Order, Long> {
    Optional<Order> findById(Long id);
    Order save(Order order);
}
```

No `deleteAll()`, no `findAll()` — callers cannot misuse the repository.

**Read-only paging repository:**

```java
public interface OrderReportRepository
        extends ListPagingAndSortingRepository<Order, Long> {
    Page<Order> findByCreatedAtBetween(Instant from, Instant to, Pageable page);
}
```

No writes possible.

**Persistence-neutral domain repository:**

```java
public interface CustomerRepository extends ListCrudRepository<Customer, UUID> {
    Optional<Customer> findByEmail(String email);
}
```

Can later swap from JPA to MongoDB / R2DBC by changing only the implementation tech.
